1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,146
Every cell in your body
carries the same DNA.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:08,577
About two metres of it, wrapped around proteins
called histones, like thread on spools.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,250
Small chemical marks on histone tails
decide which genes are read.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:19,734
Here, an enzyme adds an
activating mark, H3K27ac.

5
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,735
The nucleosomes slide apart, so
polymerase can read the gene.

6
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,227
Elsewhere, H3K27me3 packs the DNA tightly.

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:32,450
The polymerase can't get in.
The gene stays silent.

8
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,940
Zoom out, and every cell type has
its own pattern of marks.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,317
Neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes and
microglia each read different genes.

10
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:46,150
But most epigenome profiling grinds
the whole tissue together.

11
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:50,822
Every cell type's signal is
diluted into one average.

12
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,746
Peaks unique to one cell type shrink, and rare
cells like microglia nearly disappear.

13
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:03,214
NEXUS Epigenomics was founded in
Stockholm in 2024 to close these gaps,

14
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:06,231
with two ready-to-use kits:
MeTLAND and Histland.

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:13,162
MeTLAND maps DNA methylation, the
chemical tags on the DNA itself.

16
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:18,533
Histland maps histone modifications, the
marks that open and lock genes.

17
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:22,706
Two layers of the epigenome,
ready to use in your own lab.

18
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:27,327
What is your biggest gap?
Tell us in the comments.
